    {
      "short": "Fail small",
      "chapter": "technique",
      "title": "Systems thinking at global scale",
      "body": [
        "No single technique saved you here — the <em><span class=\"term\">composition</span></em> did: CDN absorbing edge reads, caches absorbing hot reads, queues absorbing write bursts, shards splitting write load, replicas and regions absorbing failures, observability telling you which of these was the problem.",
        "Notice what scale actually changed: not the concepts, but the consequences. Every small sloppiness that was survivable at 10k users became an outage at 100M."
      ],
      "callout": {
        "kind": "insight",
        "text": "You don't build a system that can't fail; you build one that fails small."
      },
